            "content": [
              {
                "type": "output_text",
                "text": "The latest Perseverance rover news is that NASA reported two major updates in mid-July 2026:\n\n- **Perseverance has driven a “marathon” on Mars**: The rover reached **26.2 miles / 42.195 km** of total driving on **Sol 1,890**, after about **five years and four months** on Mars. NASA’s Mars Reconnaissance Orbiter captured an overhead image showing the rover and its tracks just before the milestone. This is notably faster than Opportunity, which took over 11 years to cover the same distance.【5:0†source】\n\n- **It has uncovered a record of ancient asteroid impacts**: NASA/JPL says Perseverance found that a **245-foot-thick / 75-meter-thick** stack of ancient layered rock on Jezero Crater’s rim—called the **“Broom Point member”**—was likely built by repeated asteroid impacts more than **3.9 billion years ago**. Scientists say this terrain may preserve a rare record of the early solar system because Mars lacks Earth-like plate tectonics that erase ancient crustal history.【5:4†source】\n\nIn short: Perseverance is still actively exploring beyond Jezero Crater, has now driven a marathon-distance route, and is studying some of the oldest rocks ever examined by a Mars rover.",
                "annotations": [
                  {
                    "type": "url_citation",
                    "url": "https://www.sciencedaily.com/releases/2026/07/260713084858.htm",
